# tauorm.async_engine — the async story for engine.tr's Engine[C, RS],
# for callers composing DB work with other `std.async` pipelines (see
# PROPOSAL.md's "Sync and async, from day one" section).
#
# What "async" means here, honestly: Tauraro's own `async def`/`await`
# (docs/lang/16_concurrency.md) already runs an awaited call on a
# dedicated OS thread, blocking only the calling COROUTINE (not the
# process) until it finishes -- see that doc's "await f(args) launches f
# on an OS thread" line. Wrapping a plain, synchronous Engine[C, RS] call
# in a bare `async def` and awaiting it gets that OS-thread hop for free,
# composing with other awaited work in the same pipeline. This is NOT
# non-blocking I/O for either backend, and doesn't pretend to be:
#   - SQLite's C API has no async story at all -- the OS-thread hop
#     above is the same shape aiosqlite/Diesel-async use for it.
#   - taupostgres's own `async_query.tr` (`send_query`/`next_result`)
#     exists, but taupostgres's own PROPOSAL.md documents that
#     `next_result` still blocks on the socket the way `PQgetResult`
#     does on a normal connection -- not true non-blocking I/O yet.
#
# There is NO reusable, generic `async def engine_execute[C, RS](...)`
# helper in this file -- not because it's still blocked (it isn't, as of
# a later session: see point 3 below), but because nothing here has been
# changed to use one. Three real tauraroc bugs were found and root-caused
# building/finishing this phase, all now fixed upstream:
#
#   1. `await generic_fn[T](...)` never triggered monomorphization for
#      the callee at all -- the scan that finds every generic call site
#      needing a concrete copy generated (`scan_mono_expr` in
#      `codegen/c.tr`) had no case for `HirExpr.EAwait`, so it never
#      looked INSIDE an awaited expression. The `await`-machinery's own
#      thread-entry wrapper (`_tr_async_wrap_f__MONO_T`) still referenced
#      `f__MONO_T` by name -- an implicit, undeclared-function reference,
#      since that function was never emitted anywhere. FIXED (added the
#      missing case).
#   2. A second, separate bug in the SAME area: `prescan_awaits` (which
#      generates those thread-entry wrappers) ran TWICE in the overall
#      pipeline, once BEFORE monomorphized generic bodies exist and once
#      after; the first run's wrapper permanently referenced the
#      not-yet-existing function (an ordering bug, not a missing-scan
#      one). FIXED (removed the premature early call; the later one,
#      correctly positioned after monomorphized bodies AND interface
#      wraps, covers every case).
#   3. `await generic_fn[T](args)`'s overall expression TYPE was inferred
#      incorrectly (a trivial `async def f[T](x: T) -> int: return 99`
#      called as `await f[Foo](foo)` printed a `Foo` pointer's
#      description instead of the int `99`). Root-caused in a LATER
#      session: not async-specific at all -- `fname[T](args)` (a single
#      explicit type arg) never looked up the function's actual declared
#      return type in `sema.tr`, it just blindly set the call's result
#      type to the type ARGUMENT itself (only correct when the function
#      returns `T` verbatim). The identical mistake reproduces in a plain
#      SYNC call too (`mut r = get_value[Foo](foo)`), just as a loud
#      compile error instead of a silent one -- confirmed directly with
#      tauorm-unrelated repros before this fix. FIXED (the single-arg case
#      now mirrors the already-correct multi-type-arg sibling case).
#
# The proven-reliable pattern (confirmed directly, see
# tests/test_async_sqlite.tr) remains a plain, CONCRETE (non-generic)
# `async def` per concrete (C, RS) pair you actually use -- now a
# readability/explicitness choice rather than a compiler-forced one:
#
#   async def sqlite_execute(engine: Engine[SqliteConnection, SqliteResultSet], sql: str, params: Vec[str]) throws DbError -> SqliteResultSet:
#       mut r: Result[SqliteResultSet, DbError] = engine.execute(sql, params)
#       if r.is_err:
#           raise(DbError.init(r.err.code, r.err.message))
#       return r.ok
#
# Copy this one function per dialect you use (it's ~5 lines); `await
# sqlite_execute(engine, sql, params)` gets the OS-thread hop safely. A
# generic version is a viable, API-compatible upgrade now that point 3
# above is fixed -- not attempted here since nothing required it.
